<p>Each till has its own receipt settings: open it in <strong>Settings › Tills</strong> and choose how <strong>Receipts print</strong>.</p>

<h2>Counter computers that only sell</h2>
<p>Edit a till in <strong>Settings › Tills</strong> and tick <strong>Open straight into selling</strong>. On the computer set to that till,
  cashiers and stock clerks then go straight to the till when they sign in or switch user, without stopping at Home. Owners and managers
  still see Home, and the HardPoint logo at the top of the till leads there for everyone.</p>

<h2>Printing from the browser (the default)</h2>
<p>Any printer your computer can print to will work. Receipts open in the browser's print window, sized for 80 mm thermal paper. Choose the printer once and tick "don't ask again" if your browser offers it. To open the cash drawer on each receipt, turn on "open cash drawer" in the printer's driver settings.</p>

<h2>Printing silently through QZ Tray</h2>
<p><a href="https://qz.io" class="link" target="_blank" rel="noopener">QZ Tray</a> is a small free program that lets HardPoint send receipts straight to a thermal printer, without the print window, and open the cash drawer on cash sales. Install it on the till computer, then set the till to <strong>Straight to the printer with QZ Tray</strong> and type the printer's name exactly as the computer shows it. Choose the paper width: 58 mm printers fit 32 characters a line, 80 mm printers 42 or 48. The first time a till prints, QZ Tray may ask whether to allow your shop's HardPoint address: tick <strong>Remember this decision</strong> and press <strong>Allow</strong>, once per till. Each shop has its own signing certificate, so no other shop's till can print through yours.</p>

<h2>A weighing scale</h2>
<p>With a scale plugged into the till's computer (a serial cable, or a USB cable that shows up as a serial port), the till can read the
  weight of anything sold by the kilogram or gram: nails, binding wire, loose cement. Edit the till in <strong>Settings › Tills</strong> and
  choose how its <strong>Weighing scale</strong> talks. Most scales send the weight all the time; some wait to be asked, and the list names
  the usual ones. Set the <strong>speed</strong> and <strong>data format</strong> to match the scale (they're in its manual; 9600 and 8 data
  bits is the most common).</p>
<p>At the till, tap a product sold by weight and press <strong>Weigh</strong>, or press <strong>Weigh</strong> beside its quantity on the sale.
  The first time, the browser asks which port the scale is on; after that it remembers. HardPoint waits for the weight to settle, and
  won't take a reading from an empty pan. It needs Chrome or Edge on the till's computer. Without a scale, or on the offline till, type the weight as before.</p>

<h2>Barcode scanners</h2>
<p>USB and Bluetooth scanners that type like a keyboard work without any setup. Scan on the till screen and the product is added to the sale.</p>
